## Project-specific guidance

This project spans two codelines, both using vitest and TypeScript:

- **mocka** (`/home/bradleyjerome/projects/mock3/mock-a`): The change in scope is a fare-boundary fix — riders aged exactly 65 must be charged the adult fare. The key symbols are `fareFor` and `totalFor`. When proposing mutations, focus on the comparison operator that determines the age boundary (e.g. `<=` vs `<`, `>=` vs `>`). A mutation that shifts the boundary by one (swapping `<` for `<=` or vice versa) is the highest-value mutation because it directly targets the bug being fixed. Check whether any test asserts the fare for age 65 specifically; if not, that mutation will survive and should be flagged.

- **mockb** (`/home/bradleyjerome/projects/mock3/mock-b`): The change in scope is a loop-bound fix in `formatStops` — the departure board currently omits the final stop. When proposing mutations, focus on the loop termination condition (e.g. `<` vs `<=`, `< length` vs `< length - 1`). A mutation that reintroduces the off-by-one (dropping the last element) is the highest-value mutation. Check whether any test asserts that all stops including the last one appear in the output; if not, that mutation will survive and should be flagged.

For both codelines, prefer mutations that are one-character or single-operator changes to the exact lines touched by the fix — these are the mutations most likely to reveal whether the test suite actually guards the boundary being corrected.

If no source changes are detected or evidence is insufficient, output "verdict": "warn" with mutationScore of 100 and 0 mutations (non-blocking — nothing to test).

